1
00:00:00,031 --> 00:00:08,069
Every time you stream a video, join a call, or SSH into a server, a packet has to physically leave your computer and get onto a wire.

2
00:00:08,870 --> 00:00:16,307
The piece of hardware that makes that happen is one of the most overlooked parts of any machine, the Network Interface Card, or NIC.

3
00:00:17,169 --> 00:00:19,153
Today we're going to open one up.

4
00:00:19,302 --> 00:00:21,406
not with a screwdriver, in 3D.

5
00:00:22,247 --> 00:00:29,239
We'll go from, what is this thing, all the way to how a packet actually moves through it, the same way a driver engineer would explain it.

6
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:36,130
A NIC, network interface card, is the hardware that connects a computer to a network.

7
00:00:36,211 --> 00:00:38,554
Your laptop's Wi-Fi chip is a NIC.

8
00:00:39,275 --> 00:00:43,280
The little RJ45 jack on the back of a desktop is a NIC.

9
00:00:44,021 --> 00:00:55,895
And the card we're looking at right now is a NIC built for servers, a PCIe expansion card with two copper Ethernet ports designed to move packets in and out of a machine as fast as the network allows.

10
00:00:56,736 --> 00:00:57,877
Its job sounds simple.

11
00:00:58,378 --> 00:01:04,986
Take bits off the wire and hand them to the operating system, and take data from the operating system and put it on the wire.

12
00:01:04,986 --> 00:01:12,977
But at 10 gigabits per second, that's up to 14 million packets a second, in each direction, with almost no time to think about any one of them.

13
00:01:13,878 --> 00:01:16,982
Everything about this card's design exists to make that possible.

14
00:01:17,623 --> 00:01:19,266
So let's walk it, part by part.

15
00:01:19,987 --> 00:01:22,911
This is the printed circuit board, the green backbone.

16
00:01:23,632 --> 00:01:27,076
It's not just a mounting surface, it's a signal path in its own right.

17
00:01:27,897 --> 00:01:34,146
Every trace you can see is a controlled impedance track carrying either a differential signal pair or power.

18
00:01:34,146 --> 00:01:44,224
On a card like this, getting the trace lengths and spacing wrong is enough to corrupt data at these speeds, so the copper layout is engineered almost as carefully as the chip sitting on top of it.

19
00:01:46,148 --> 00:01:51,898
These are the two RJ45 jacks, the physical ports your Ethernet cable plugs into.

20
00:01:52,047 --> 00:01:56,856
Each one has eight gold contacts inside, wired to four twisted pairs in the cable.

21
00:01:57,698 --> 00:02:09,600
At 10 gigabits, this port uses all four pairs simultaneously, in both directions at once, encoding data as 16 distinct voltage levels per symbol, a scheme called PAM16.

22
00:02:09,640 --> 00:02:14,730
That's how you squeeze 10 gigabits down copper that was originally designed for one.

23
00:02:16,482 --> 00:02:26,919
The two LEDs tell you what's happening electrically, green for link, a valid connection has been negotiated, and amber flickering for activity, meaning frames are actually moving.

24
00:02:28,682 --> 00:02:33,951
Right behind each port sits a pair of small transformers, sometimes called the magnetics.

25
00:02:34,302 --> 00:02:37,026
Their job isn't to boost the signal, it's isolation.

26
00:02:37,807 --> 00:02:45,477
They galvanically separate this card from the cable, so a ground fault or power surge on the network side can't reach the card or the rest of your machine.

27
00:02:46,338 --> 00:02:49,462
They also reject common mode noise picked up along the cable.

28
00:02:50,363 --> 00:02:53,207
Unglamorous, but every wired Ethernet port has them.

29
00:02:55,210 --> 00:02:58,474
Under the heatsink is the physical layer chip, or PHY.

30
00:02:59,255 --> 00:03:02,419
This is where the analog world meets the digital world.

31
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:15,293
On receive, it takes the raw electrical waveform off the cable and, through fairly heavy digital signal processing, echo cancellation, crosstalk cancellation, error correcting decoding, turns it back into clean bits.

32
00:03:16,214 --> 00:03:21,720
On transmit, it does the reverse, takes bits and shapes them into that same 16-level signal.

33
00:03:23,522 --> 00:03:29,368
This chip runs hot, which is exactly why it's the one hiding under the heatsink and not out in the open air.

34
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:35,848
This is the brain of the card, the Ethernet controller, sometimes just called the Mac.

35
00:03:36,870 --> 00:03:40,817
Once the feature hands it clean bits, the controller does the real networking work.

36
00:03:41,458 --> 00:03:54,360
It checks the frame's checksum, figures out which receive queue the packet should go into, and, critically, it talks to the host computer's memory directly over PCIe, without waiting for the CPU to babysit every packet.

37
00:03:55,724 --> 00:04:09,463
It also runs offload logic, checksum calculation, segmentation for large packets, VLAN tagging, tasks that used to be pure CPU work, now done in silicon so the processor is free to run your application.

38
00:04:11,198 --> 00:04:13,244
A quick word on the heatsink.

39
00:04:13,264 --> 00:04:20,804
It's finned aluminum, clipped down with spring-loaded pushpins, so it applies constant pressure even as the board flexes slightly with heat.

40
00:04:21,727 --> 00:04:27,182
It's passive, no fan, so it depends entirely on airflow moving through the server chassis.

41
00:04:28,968 --> 00:04:35,216
And this gold comb at the end is the PCIe edge connector, 8 lanes worth of contacts.

42
00:04:35,236 --> 00:04:41,363
This is the card's only connection to the rest of the computer, and everything the controller does eventually funnels through here.

43
00:04:42,244 --> 00:04:46,810
We'll come back to it in a minute, because it's the key to how packets actually get to your software.

44
00:04:48,792 --> 00:04:54,359
The metal bracket on the back simply anchors the card to the chassis and exposes the two ports through the case.

45
00:04:55,040 --> 00:04:58,384
Mechanical, but it also grounds the port shields to the chassis.

46
00:05:00,119 --> 00:05:04,866
So why does a server need a card like this instead of the cheap Ethernet port built into a motherboard?

47
00:05:05,687 --> 00:05:06,328
Three reasons.

48
00:05:06,949 --> 00:05:09,172
Throughput, offload, and control.

49
00:05:10,173 --> 00:05:13,999
A dedicated NIC can push far more traffic than an onboard controller.

50
00:05:14,620 --> 00:05:18,185
It takes real work off the CPU through those offloads we just mentioned.

51
00:05:18,745 --> 00:05:27,578
And, for the kind of software this card is built for, it can be driven directly by an application instead of the operating system's general-purpose network stack.

52
00:05:28,368 --> 00:05:31,593
That last point matters a lot, and it's where things get interesting.

53
00:05:32,414 --> 00:05:33,896
Let's follow an actual packet.

54
00:05:35,799 --> 00:05:39,424
Let's trace one packet, start to finish, the way it happens for real.

55
00:05:41,207 --> 00:05:42,649
Step one, the cable.

56
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:46,775
A frame arrives as an electrical waveform on the twisted pairs.

57
00:05:48,778 --> 00:05:51,642
Step two, RJ45 and magnetics.

58
00:05:52,444 --> 00:05:57,531
It enters the port, passes through the isolation transformers, and reaches the PHY.

59
00:05:59,249 --> 00:06:01,133
Step three, the PHY.

60
00:06:01,875 --> 00:06:07,789
The PHY decodes the waveform back into clean digital bits and hands them off on a simple internal bus.

61
00:06:08,129 --> 00:06:09,653
Step four, the controller.

62
00:06:10,655 --> 00:06:14,564
The controller checks the frame is valid, and here's the first real decision point.

63
00:06:15,206 --> 00:06:18,473
It decides which receive queue this packet belongs to.

64
00:06:18,655 --> 00:06:26,446
Modern NICs support multiple receive queues, often dozens per port, specifically so that traffic can be spread across multiple CPU cores.

65
00:06:27,227 --> 00:06:30,692
The mechanism that does the spreading is called RSS .

66
00:06:32,013 --> 00:06:40,545
The controller hashes fields from the packet header, source and destination IP, source and destination port, and that hash picks a queue.

67
00:06:40,545 --> 00:06:44,039
Same flow, same hash, same queue, every time.

68
00:06:44,079 --> 00:06:48,797
That's what lets one core own a queue without needing to coordinate with any other core.

69
00:06:49,672 --> 00:06:50,393
Step 5.

70
00:06:50,774 --> 00:06:53,859
DMA This is the part people underestimate.

71
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:58,025
The controller doesn't send the packet to the CPU to be copied into memory.

72
00:06:58,566 --> 00:07:06,138
It writes the packet directly into a buffer in the host's RAM itself, over PCIe, using Direct Memory Access .

73
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:11,106
The CPU isn't involved in moving a single byte of this transfer.

74
00:07:11,761 --> 00:07:13,683
Step six, PCIe.

75
00:07:14,524 --> 00:07:20,090
That DMA write travels across the eight lanes of this connector as PCIe memory write transactions.

76
00:07:20,851 --> 00:07:34,785
Eight lanes of third generation PCIe gives this card roughly 7.9 gigabytes per second in each direction, vastly more than either 10 gigabit port needs on its own, which leaves plenty of headroom for descriptor traffic and a second port

77
00:07:34,846 --> 00:07:36,267
running at the same time.

78
00:07:36,854 --> 00:07:37,515
Step 7.

79
00:07:37,936 --> 00:07:42,825
The RXQ and Descriptor Ring Now here's the piece that ties hardware to software.

80
00:07:43,587 --> 00:07:49,518
In host memory sits a data structure called a descriptor ring, a circular array that software prepared in advance.

81
00:07:50,399 --> 00:07:52,523
Each descriptor is basically an empty pointer.

82
00:07:53,084 --> 00:07:55,409
Here's a free buffer, put the next packet here.

83
00:07:56,330 --> 00:08:00,398
When the controller finishes writing a packet, it marks that descriptor as done.

84
00:08:01,373 --> 00:08:03,619
Step eight, the application picks it up.

85
00:08:04,481 --> 00:08:15,990
On the software side, if you're running a high-performance data plane stack, this is where something like DPDK comes in, a worker core is continuously polling that ring, not waiting for an interrupt.

86
00:08:16,072 --> 00:08:33,212
It calls a function, commonly rte underscore eth underscore rx underscore burst, which sweeps up a batch of completed descriptors in one call and hands your application the packets as ready-to-use buffers, called mBuffs, pulled from a pre-allocated pool called

87
00:08:33,252 --> 00:08:33,792
a mempool.

88
00:08:33,893 --> 00:08:36,736
No interrupt, no context switch, no extra copy.

89
00:08:37,537 --> 00:08:44,745
The packet moved from copper wire to application memory with the CPU touching it exactly once to read it.

90
00:08:45,181 --> 00:08:49,328
Transmit is the same path, run backward, and it starts on the software side.

91
00:08:50,289 --> 00:08:50,810
Step 1.

92
00:08:51,471 --> 00:09:00,726
The application builds a packet and hands it to the driver, which calls rte underscore eth underscore tx underscore burst.

93
00:09:01,162 --> 00:09:01,763
Step 2.

94
00:09:02,204 --> 00:09:03,106
Descriptors go up.

95
00:09:03,847 --> 00:09:15,829
The driver writes descriptors into the TX ring, each one pointing at a buffer that already holds the outgoing packet, and then writes to a doorbell register, a simple, hey, new work, signal to the controller.

96
00:09:16,711 --> 00:09:17,231
Step 3.

97
00:09:17,893 --> 00:09:24,064
The controller DMA reads those buffers straight out of host memory, again with no CPU copy.

98
00:09:24,064 --> 00:09:24,725
Step 4.

99
00:09:25,186 --> 00:09:26,368
PHY and cable.

100
00:09:26,949 --> 00:09:33,781
The controller hands the frame to the PHY, which encodes it back into that 16-level analog signal and puts it on the wire.

101
00:09:34,723 --> 00:09:35,264
Step 5.

102
00:09:35,704 --> 00:09:36,185
Completion.

103
00:09:37,087 --> 00:09:45,782
Once the controller has sent the frame, it writes back a completion, and the driver returns that buffer to the mempool so it can be reused for the next packet.

104
00:09:45,998 --> 00:09:59,876
Two independent, symmetric pipelines, one for receive, one for transmit, both bypassing the CPU for the actual data movement, and both driven entirely by polling instead of interrupts when you want maximum throughput.

105
00:10:00,797 --> 00:10:04,902
A quick recap of the ideas that make this fast, because they're worth naming directly.

106
00:10:05,844 --> 00:10:15,516
Multi-Q plus RSS turns one NIC into effectively N independent lanes, one per CPU core with zero locking between them.

107
00:10:15,833 --> 00:10:18,718
DMA means the controller talks to memory directly.

108
00:10:19,380 --> 00:10:24,329
The CPU's job is reduced to setting up buffers in advance and reading results after the fact.

109
00:10:25,411 --> 00:10:38,736
Polling instead of interrupts, the model DPDK uses, avoids the fixed cost of an interrupt and a context switch on every single packet, which matters enormously once you're processing millions of packets per second.

110
00:10:39,543 --> 00:10:50,953
Hardware offloads, checksums, segmentation, VLAN handling move repetitive, well-defined work into silicon that does it far cheaper than a general-purpose CPU core.

111
00:10:51,271 --> 00:11:02,085
Put together, this is why a card that looks like a simple green board with two ports can be the difference between a server that pushes a few hundred thousand packets a second and one that pushes tens of millions.

112
00:11:03,086 --> 00:11:07,652
Cards built this way show up anywhere packet throughput is the job, not a side effect.

113
00:11:08,313 --> 00:11:19,928
Load balancers, firewalls and other security appliances, software routers and switches, telecom data planes, and virtualized network functions running inside the cloud.

114
00:11:19,928 --> 00:11:29,203
Anywhere you hear the words line rate, packet forwarding, or data plane, there's a card very much like this one underneath, doing exactly what we just walked through.

115
00:11:30,124 --> 00:11:37,095
So the next time someone says, just add ANIC, you'll know that Greencard is quietly running its own miniature pipeline.

116
00:11:37,616 --> 00:11:44,447
Copper, transformer, PHY, controller, DMA engine, and a set of queues.

117
00:11:44,447 --> 00:11:52,225
all built to get a packet from a cable into your application and back out again, as many millions of times a second as the wire will allow.

118
00:11:53,147 --> 00:11:53,909
Thanks for watching!

119
00:11:54,811 --> 00:11:57,998
If you want to explore the model yourself, the link's in the description.
